<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
    :recorder library manifest. Under AGP 8 the namespace comes from build.gradle.kts, so no
    package attribute is needed here.

    RECORD_AUDIO is declared here so it merges into every consumer. D-11: the library only CHECKS
    the permission (Context.checkSelfPermission); the HOST app requests it, because a library has
    no Activity to request from — the same contract asymmetry :stt documents.

    FOREGROUND_SERVICE and FOREGROUND_SERVICE_MICROPHONE are declared here for RecorderForegroundService
    (D-02 Arm A, AUDIO-03), which the <application>/<service> element below declares.
    CONSEQUENCE, STATED PLAINLY (design decision 11, REVIEW-OC-07): every consumer of :recorder
    inherits BOTH foreground-service permissions by manifest merge, whether or not it ever binds
    RecorderForegroundService — a record-only consumer that never touches the service still gets
    these two permissions merged into its manifest. The alternative — publishing the service as a
    separate opt-in artifact so a record-only consumer inherits nothing — was considered and
    rejected: it would add a fifth publishable module to the release script's cross-module
    version-agreement gate, force a consumer to pin two coordinates instead of one, and double the
    surface an immutable tag freezes, all to spare a consumer two permissions that grant it no
    capability beyond the RECORD_AUDIO it already needs to record. Accepted, and stated here rather
    than left for a consumer to discover in a merged-manifest diff.

    The service is declared non-exported below — no other app on the device may bind to it
    (T-142-06). See RecorderForegroundService's own KDoc for the binder-only API contract, the
    armed-teardown rule, and the threading contract.

    DELIBERATELY WITHOUT :stt's <queries> entry for android.speech.RecognitionService. Recorder
    never touches recognition, so it has no need to query for a recognizer package. This is a
    genuine, if partial, strengthening over the single-AAR era (08-RESEARCH.md's Pitfall 5 /
    Assumption A4 flagged that manifest merging is unconditional and R8-independent): a
    :recorder-ONLY consumer's merged manifest now genuinely never gains the recognition <queries>
    entry. A consumer that ALSO depends on :stt still gets it from :stt's own manifest — expected,
    unrelated to this guarantee.
-->
<manifest xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android">

    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.RECORD_AUDIO" />
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.FOREGROUND_SERVICE" />
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.FOREGROUND_SERVICE_MICROPHONE" />

    <application>
        <service
            android:name=".RecorderForegroundService"
            android:exported="false"
            android:foregroundServiceType="microphone" />
    </application>

</manifest>
